Not in a way that anyone can explain linguistically.

The usual in-universe fan handwave explanation is
that the song is very old, and the language has
changed out from under it, so that the meaning is
no longer clear if you only know modern Klingon.
The truth is that the writers of the episode just
threw random syllables together.

A meaning is given for the first two sentences only:
"Fire streaks the heavens.  Battle has begun."

> > * "Hajimemashite.  Minamototomoushimasu.
> > Douzoyoroshikuonegaiitashimasu."
>
> Is this something in Japanese?

Something along the lines of, "It is a great honor
to meet you.  Your servant is called Minamoto.
I am not worthy to stand in your presence."
This is by necessity an extremely loose
translation.  Polite Japanese greetings are
mostly protocol and do not translate well.
The significance of the name Minamoto is
also a rather long story.


